A maintainer on the project eugenemalaschuk-source/arch-linter-net (C#) filed this issue about hand-rolled graph machinery causing pain:

Issue #461: [TASK][AI] Performance: Reproduce and attribute large-solution duration regression

Parent story: #19
Release target: milestone 7 / v0.9.0 Performance and scale
Downstream prepared-state story: #492
Related benchmark foundation: #502

## Goal

Reproduce and attribute a sharp architecture-validation duration increase observed in a real multi-project ASP.NET consumer CI run, then convert the observed scaling pattern into anonymized/synthetic evidence that can safely drive ArchLinterNet optimizations.

This is an **evidence and attribution task**, not permission to implement a guessed optimization. The task must determine whether the increase is inside ArchLinterNet at all, and if so identify the dominant phase/hot path before implementation follow-ups proceed.

## Privacy / public-backlog boundary

- Do not name or link the consumer repository, workflow run, proprietary product, namespaces or internal topology.
- Consumer evidence may be used locally to reproduce the problem, but committed fixtures and public issue/PR text must be synthetic or anonymized.
- Preserve only the structural dimensions necessary to reproduce the scaling behavior.

## Problem statement

The consumer executes ArchLinterNet inside a larger CI orchestration step rather than as a dedicated GitHub Actions step. The outer step duration increased sharply, but that aggregate timing mixes architecture validation with surrounding startup/build/container/orchestration cost.

The same acceptance workflow may also invoke several independent ArchLinterNet processes over one unchanged build state. That repeated-process shape is now represented by focused story #492, but #492 implementation remains blocked until this task plus #493 prove that the repeated preparation/fact work is material.

Therefore the first required result is an attribution boundary:

1. prove that ArchLinterNet's own wall-clock duration regressed, or
2. prove that the increase is external to ArchLinterNet, or
3. identify that current profiling is insufficient and add the smallest instrumentation needed to decide between 1 and 2.

No algorithmic root cause may be asserted from outer CI step duration alone.

## Investigation phases

### 1. Establish the consumer timing boundary

Capture timestamps/evidence around the actual ArchLinterNet invocation inside the larger orchestration path.

Separate, where applicable:

- process/container/tool startup;
- restore/build/`--ensure-built` or build-state verification;
- policy load/schema/import/composition;
- workspace/project/assembly discovery;
- post-build metadata preparation;
- assembly/type/reference loading and indexing;
- dependency graph/index construction;
- contract evaluation;
- finding identity/aggregation/order;
- baseline/public-API comparison where part of the workflow;
- output serialization/publication;
- cache eligibility/lookup/hit/miss/population.

When the workflow runs multiple independent ArchLinterNet commands against the same unchanged build, identify duplicated preparation/fact work per process rather than reporting only one aggregate total.

Record current tool/runtime/environment version and whether the run is cold, warm, cache-hit or cache-miss.

### 2. Compare against stable baselines

Use multiple comparable runs rather than one before/after point.

For each useful run, capture at least:

- total inner ArchLinterNet duration;
- major phase durations available from `analysis-profile/v1`;
- project/assembly/type/reference/dependency-edge counts;
- contract count and finding count;
- cache state;
- bounded/sequential execution mode;
- material environment/runtime differences.

Identify which dimension changed at the same time as the duration increase.

### 3. Determine whether existing profiling is sufficient

If `analysis-profile/v1` cannot attribute the dominant cost, add the smallest backward-compatible evidence needed for this investigation.

Candidate counters/timings, only where needed:

- project/metadata preparation count and duration;
- post-`--ensure-built` preparation count and duration;
- graph/index build count and duration;
- evaluator/family duration;
- transitive/reachability traversal count;
- visited node/edge count;
- witness-path reconstruction/materialization count;
- finding-candidate and identity-attachment count;
- type/reference scan count;
- public API surface scan count where relevant;
- allocated bytes / peak managed memory or equivalent stable memory signal;
- bounded partition/work count.

Do not add high-cardinality per-type/per-symbol telemetry to ordinary output.

### 4. Isolate scaling dimensions

Determine whether cost primarily scales with one or more of:

- number of independent ArchLinterNet processes over the same build;
- number of projects;
- assemblies;
- source files;
- types;
- references/dependency edges;
- contracts;
- source/target selector cardinality;
- findings;
- alternate dependency-path multiplicity;
- cache invalidation/recomputation;
- output volume.

### 5. Test explicit complexity/reuse hypotheses

Test, where relevant to the measured hot path, rather than assuming:

- repeated project/build/fact preparation across independent processes;
- repeated graph/index/fact construction per contract;
- source × target × traversal amplification;
- repeated transitive/reachability traversal for equivalent roots/modes;
- all-path enumeration/materialization when one deterministic witness path would satisfy diagnostics;
- quadratic or worse finding identity/aggregation;
- repeated type/reference scanning across evaluator families;
- pathological cache miss/ineligibility behavior;
- parallel partition overhead or allocation dominating useful work.

The repeated-process hypothesis routes to #492/#493 only if measurement shows it is material. It is not assumed true merely because multiple commands exist.

### 6. Produce a minimal public reproducer

Translate the consumer-shaped scaling behavior into a synthetic/anonymized fixture or benchmark.

Prefer the smallest topology that still reproduces the growth. Candidate generators:

- 

Write a small self-contained Jac demo program that models the essential scenario from this issue natively: pick the 2-4 core entity types as node archetypes with typed has fields, connect instances with edges (typed edges where the relationship carries meaning), and implement walker(s) that perform the traversal the issue complains about (e.g. recursion over parents/children, cascade delete or invalidation, dependency ordering, cycle detection, reachability). Wire a small demonstration graph in a `with entry` block and spawn the walker(s). No I/O, no external libraries, no pytest blocks.

```jac
"""ArchLinterNet attribution demo: model repeated-process preparation work.

The consumer shapes a CI step that runs several independent ArchLinterNet
processes against one unchanged build state. The cost split we must
attribute is:

  * per-process startup / project discovery / metadata preparation
  * per-process post-build preparation
  * per-process dependency graph + index construction
  * per-process contract evaluation (traversal)

This program models that four-phase shape with a typed graph and a
walker that walks each "process" once, recording per-phase duration so
the dominant hot path is observable from one self-contained run.
"""

node Project {
    has name: str = "";
    has source_files: int = 0;
    has types: int = 0;
}

node Contract {
    has rule: str = "";
    has selector_cardinality: int = 0;
}

edge DependsOn {
    has kind: str = "";
}

edge Evaluates {
    has mode: str = "";
}

node PhaseTimer {
    has phase: str = "";
    has started_at: float = 0.0;
    has finished_at: float = 0.0;

    def duration_ms() -> float {
        return (self.finished_at - self.started_at) * 1000.0;
    }
}

walker ProcessRun {
    has pid: int = 0;
    has project_count: int = 0;
    has contract_count: int = 0;
    has edge_count: int = 0;
    has findings: int = 0;
    has phase_log: list = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        # Phase 1: startup + project discovery -- record a timer node
        # then walk on to discover projects.
        discovery = PhaseTimer(phase="discovery");
        here ++> discovery;
        self.phase_log.append(discovery);
        visit [-->];
    }

    can discover with Project entry {
        self.project_count += 1;
        visit [-->];
    }

    can evaluate with Contract entry {
        self.contract_count += 1;
        # The repeated edge traversal here is the per-process cost we
        # want to make visible; if it dominates, that is the
        # repeated-graph hypothesis from the issue.
        targets = [-->];
        self.edge_count += len(targets);
        self.findings += here.selector_cardinality;
        visit [-->];
    }
}

walker Attribution {
    has process_count: int = 0;
    has total_findings: int = 0;
    has per_process_durations: list = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        # Fan out one ProcessRun per "independent ArchLinterNet process"
        # the consumer CI step would launch.
        self.process_count = 3;
        print(
            f"Attribution: processes={self.process_count}, "
            f"phase_records_observed={len(self.per_process_durations)}, "
            f"discovery_phase_total_findings={self.total_findings}"
        );
        visit [-->];
    }

    can attribute with PhaseTimer entry {
        # Each timer represents one observed phase of one process run.
        self.per_process_durations.append(here.phase);
        if here.phase == "discovery" {
            self.total_findings += 1;
        }
        visit [-->];
    }
}

with entry {
    # Synthetic consumer topology: 3 projects, each depending on the next,
    # plus 2 contracts evaluated across the whole dependency closure.
    a = Project(name="Alpha", source_files=120, types=900);
    b = Project(name="Beta", source_files=80, types=600);
    c = Project(name="Gamma", source_files=40, types=300);
    root ++> a ++> b ++> c;

    a +>:DependsOn(kind="project-ref"):+> b;
    b +>:DependsOn(kind="project-ref"):+> c;

    rule_a = Contract(rule="no-circular-deps", selector_cardinality=4);
    rule_b = Contract(rule="layer-discipline", selector_cardinality=6);
    root ++> rule_a ++> rule_b;

    rule_a +>:Evaluates(mode="transitive"):+> a;
    rule_a +>:Evaluates(mode="transitive"):+> b;
    rule_a +>:Evaluates(mode="transitive"):+> c;
    rule_b +>:Evaluates(mode="single-pass"):+> a;
    rule_b +>:Evaluates(mode="single-pass"):+> c;

    # Run the per-process walker three times against the same unchanged
    # build state -- this is the repeated-process shape from the issue.
    for i in range(3) {
        run = ProcessRun(pid=i);
        root spawn run;
        total_ms = 0.0;
        for timer in run.phase_log {
            total_ms += timer.duration_ms();
        }
        print(
            f"  -> after process {i}: findings={run.findings}, "
            f"contracts={run.contract_count}, edges_touched={run.edge_count}, "
            f"phase_total_ms={total_ms:.2f}"
        );
    }

    # Attribute by walking the recorded phase timers.
    attr = Attribution();
    root spawn attr;
}
